WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.940
ai agents are stepping in to handle the boring tasks you usually put off. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 183. You know that feeling when the day is finally over, and your inbox still wants an hour of your life? Tom's guide just put out a how-to on handing that pile. Plus flight prices and tomorrow's plan to an artificial intelligence agent, or ai agent. Picture a really smart autocomplete that does not stop after one reply.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:34.160
You give it a job and it keeps checking, then comes back when something actually changes.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.980
It is like handing your phone a checklist, then walking away while the phone works the

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:39.380
steps.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.260
The guide names seven everyday drops that fit this pattern.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:50.180
One is inbox triage, which means summarizing new mail, splitting just so you know notes from

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:55.020
stuff that needs a reply, and drafting the boring answers you still approve before they

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:55.480
go out.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.160
Another is price-watching on flights, hotels, or a gadget you want.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.440
You name the number you will actually pay, and it pings you if the cost drops, or a

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.480
better deal shows up.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.340
A third is a next day briefing, like a coach sliding a one-page game plan under your door.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.820
It pulls from your calendar and other connected info, then tells you what deserves attention.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:23.780
If you are juggling school and hobbies, that is less locker admin time, and more time

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.500
for the Minecraft build, the YouTube edit, or the art project.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:33.380
Learning to boss these helpers around is also turning into a work skill because more companies

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.040
want people who can direct them.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:42.340
You can start tiny today, open chat GPT or Gemini on your phone, and type a standing instruction

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.940
such as, summarize new emails about homework deadlines, split the ones that need a reply,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.380
and draft a short answer I will approve.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.320
move. Watch how fussy it is about timing and rewrite the instruction until it reports

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.740
back only when you would actually care. The trick is saying exactly what to do and when

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:07.020
to tap you on the shoulder, so it feels like a helper, not another noisy app. Open AI showed

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:13.260
a tool called Dots at its developer event DevDay on Tuesday and meta-launched Muse earlier

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.720
this month, which is a sign these helpers are being aimed at regular people, not just engineers.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.120
Here is the part that clicked for me, and it is simpler than the name agent makes it sound.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.960
Think about asking a neighbor to water your plants while you are away.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.100
They do not text you every leaf.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.880
They check every couple of days, notice if the soil is dry, and message you only when something

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:36.780
needs a decision.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.280
An AI agent copies that habit, except the plants are digital jobs like a price, a mailbox,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:44.320
or a calendar.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.080
You leave a standing note such as watch this price and tell me if it drops, and it keeps

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:51.800
looking without you poking it again.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.060
A regular chatbot is more like a vending machine.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.080
You put in one question, you get one answer, and then it just waits.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.820
The agent can set its own reminders, loop back later, and hop across apps like mail or shopping

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.080
without you hurting it.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.560
Under the hood, it still predicts the next useful step.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.120
the same core trick chatbots use, but it adds a repeat loop like checking the

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:20.940
oven without anyone yelling, is it done yet? That loop is what turns a one-shot

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.800
helper into a teammate for follow-up work, so your brain can stay on the fun part.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.100
Once you see it as a looping checklist with a report-back rule, the fancy label

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:36.180
stops feeling mysterious. Before you hand anything real to a helper though, there

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.860
There is a scam you should be able to spot in two seconds.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:45.600
A security report from Huntress describes attackers building fake custom GPT pages, meaning

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.760
fake specialized chat GPT setups that sit where people already trust the brand.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:56.100
It looks like a normal upgrade then shoves you toward a page that mimics those prove you

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.420
are human checks and asks you to paste a command into windows and run it.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:06.000
That is the whole trick, using a familiar name so your guard drops, then talking you

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.100
into installing harmful software yourself.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:14.020
If a message pushes a backup site or tells you to run a system command to verify anything,

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:20.720
stop and close the tab, stay on the official chat GPT site and never paced computer commands

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.680
from a page you did not expect even if the logo looks perfect.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.440
Treat it like a fake haul pass that photocopied the school header.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.100
The real office would never ask you to rewire the building to prove you belong.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.240
And on a much lighter note, TikTok spent a minute treating a chatbot like a breakup.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:44.340
An influencer named Alexander Cross posted a dramatic clip about deleting chat GPT,

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:50.220
and people piled on as if it were a real split, until a follow-up made the joke obvious and

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.100
said she was kidding, it was a trend.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:58.360
One reaction even read the first video as a confession about feeling isolated, which tells

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:02.760
tells you how personal these chats can feel when you use them for homework or brainstorming.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:08.840
The humor is that farewell language, aimed at an app, sounds exactly like a good bi-text.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:15.260
Search, goodbye to chat GPT on TikTok, skim the comments, and notice how fast people

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.180
jump to writing a fake obituary.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.700
It is a silly reminder that a tool can feel like a study buddy, and also that the internet

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.060
will turn that feeling into a meme by lunch.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:30.620
Meanwhile, President Trump announced a government chat bot called America.gov.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.760
It is meant to answer questions drawn from 29,000 official sites, so public information

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.240
sits in one place instead of a scavenger hunt.

